  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nTODAY-THURSDAY: A cutoff low is lifting out of the four-corners \nregion and into the Plains. Ahead of this feature, a surface low has \ndeveloped over the Central Rockies. Continued rain and storms will \nimpact portions of the forecast area, with the heaviest rainfall \nexpected over south-central and southeastern Kansas where a Flood \nWatch remains in effect through 18Z Thursday. The heaviest \nprecipitation chances will remain south of the forecast area along \nthe surface boundary. The low pressure will traverse the state on \nThursday with rain chances ending quickly behind it. Dense cloud \ncover will linger over the state today and Thursday, finally \nclearing on Thursday night from west to east. Under the thick \nstratus and on and off rain chances, temperatures will remain quite \ncool. Continued the trend of undercutting NBM highs as we have seem \nlimited diurnal heating so far today. Temperatures today will \ngenerally reach into the 50s and 60s, with a couple of 70s in far \nsoutheast Kansas. There will be minimal variability in overnight \ntemperatures, with lows generally in the 50s to lower 60s. Highs may \nbe able to rebound slightly on Thursday with temperatures reaching \ninto the 60s and low 70s. Breezy northwesterly winds will develop on \nThursday afternoon, lasting into the overnight hours.\n\nFRIDAY-SATURDAY: High pressure will begin strengthening behind the \nexiting low to the east. The tightening pressure gradient across the \nPlains will lead to breezy conditions on Friday. Northerly to \nnorthwesterly winds between 20-25 mph are expected with gusts up to \n35 mph possible. As this Canadian high builds into the region, crisp \nfall temperatures are expected for both Friday and Saturday. Highs \neach afternoon are expected to reach into the middle to upper 50s. \nOvernight lows for Friday night into Saturday morning are expected \nto fall into the 30s areawide as the high pressure is centered over \nthe state. Light winds and clear skies will likely lead to frost \nconcerns, especially in central Kansas.\n\nSUNDAY-NEXT WEEK: Ridging will begin to build into the region over \nthe weekend with temperatures rebounding slightly by Sunday. Highs \non Sunday will reach into the middle to upper 60s under mostly clear \nskies and light winds. On Sunday night into Monday, a weak impulse \nwill eject over the Plains. It is unlikely that this system will \ncarry with it enough moisture to overcome the anomalously dry air \nmass that will be in place to manufacture any rain chances. It looks \nlike the most likely impacts will be slight increases in cloud \ncover. Temperatures will then continue to warm into early next week \nwith near normal temperatures expected along with mostly clear \nconditions. At the tail end of the forecast period for mid next \nweek, model guidance is indicating the development of another \nwestern US mid/upper level trough that could usher in additional \nrain chances. Significant uncertainty remains regarding timing and \nexact precip chances, we will continue to fine tune the details as \nwe get closer.",
